  "faqQ1": "How accurate are the speed tests?",
  "faqA1": "NetChui uses high-performance edge servers (Cloudflare, Tele2, etc.) to ensure low latency and high accuracy. However, results can vary based on network congestion and your device's hardware.",
  "faqQ2": "What is Jitter?",
  "faqA2": "Jitter is the variation in time delay between data packets sent over a network. Lower jitter means a more stable connection, which is crucial for gaming and video calls.",
  "faqQ3": "Does this app use a lot of data?",
  "faqA3": "Standard tests use about 20-50MB. Quick tests use less, while 'Accurate' tests can use over 100MB to fully saturate your connection.",
  "faqQ4": "Why is my speed lower than my plan?",
  "faqA4": "Speeds advertised by ISPs are 'up to' values. Factors like Wi-Fi interference, distance from the router, number of connected devices, and background app usage can lower results.",
  "faqQ5": "2.4GHz vs 5GHz Wi-Fi?",
  "faqA5": "2.4GHz has better range but lower speeds. 5GHz (and 6GHz) offers much higher speeds but doesn't travel through walls as effectively. Use 5GHz when close to the router.",
  "faqQ6": "Why is Upload usually slower?",
  "faqA6": "Most residential internet plans are 'asymmetric', meaning they prioritize download bandwidth for streaming and browsing over upload bandwidth.",
  "faqQ7": "How can I lower my Ping?",
  "faqA7": "To lower ping, use a wired Ethernet connection if possible, move closer to your router, close background downloads, and ensure you're testing against a server physically close to you.",
  "faqQ8": "What is a 'Good' speed?",
  "faqA8": "For basic browsing, 5-10 Mbps is enough. For 4K streaming, you need 25+ Mbps. For a smooth multi-user household, 100+ Mbps is recommended.",
  "faqQ9": "Does NetChui store my location?",
  "faqA9": "No. NetChui uses your IP address to estimate your ISP and general region for the test, but we do not track or store your precise GPS location.",
  "faqQ10": "What is 'Loaded' Latency?",
  "faqA10": "Loaded latency measures your ping while your connection is being used (during download or upload). If this is much higher than your normal ping, you may experience lag while others in your house are streaming or downloading.",
  "faqQ11": "Can I use NetChui for LAN speeds?",
  "faqA11": "NetChui is primarily for internet speed. To test LAN speeds between devices, use the LAN Scanner tool to identify devices, but actual transfer speeds depend on your internal hardware capabilities.",
  "faqQ12": "How do I export my data?",
  "faqA12": "Go to Settings > Activity Logs > Export All Data. You can save your history and diagnostic logs as a JSON file or generate a PDF report from the Report screen.",
  "faqQ13": "Will a VPN affect my results?",
  "faqA13": "Yes. A VPN encrypts your traffic and routes it through another server, which usually increases latency (ping) and may reduce overall download/upload speeds.",
  "faqQ14": "Mbps vs MB/s: What's the difference?",
  "faqA14": "Mbps stands for Megabits per second (used for internet speeds). MB/s stands for Megabytes per second (used for file sizes). 8 Mbps = 1 MB/s.",
  "faqQ15": "How often should I run a test?",
  "faqA15": "We recommend testing at different times of the day (morning, afternoon, evening) to see how peak usage in your area affects your performance.",
  "faqQ16": "Wi-Fi vs. Internet Speed?",
  "faqA16": "Your Wi-Fi speed is the connection between your device and router. Internet speed is the connection between your router and the world. NetChui measures the end-to-end internet speed from your device.",
  "faqQ17": "Can weather affect speed?",
  "faqA17": "Yes, especially for satellite and fixed-wireless connections. Heavy rain or snow (rain fade) can degrade signal quality and reduce speeds or increase latency.",
  "faqQ18": "What is Packet Loss?",
  "faqA18": "Packet loss occurs when data units fail to reach their destination. It can cause 'stuttering' in video calls or gaming. NetChui identifies stability issues in the Live Monitor.",
  "faqQ19": "Why use DNS Speed Test?",
  "faqA19": "DNS is like a phonebook for the internet. Faster DNS means your browser finds websites quicker, making the internet feel much more responsive.",
  "faqQ20": "What is Bufferbloat?",
  "faqA20": "Bufferbloat is high latency that occurs when there is a lot of traffic on your network. It's caused by routers buffering too much data. NetChui's 'Loaded Latency' helps identify this.",
  "faqQ21": "Why is my ping different in games?",
  "faqA21": "Game pings depend on the location of the game's specific servers. NetChui tests against general edge servers. If the game server is further away, your ping will be higher.",
  "faqQ22": "Can I trust 'Free Wi-Fi'?",
  "faqA22": "Open Wi-Fi networks can be risky. We recommend using NetChui's Port Scan and Security Audit to check for common vulnerabilities when connected to public networks.",
  "faqQ23": "Does my device age matter?",
  "faqA23": "Yes. Older phones or laptops may have slower Wi-Fi chips (e.g., Wi-Fi 4 instead of Wi-Fi 6), which limits the maximum speed they can handle regardless of your internet plan.",
  "faqQ24": "How does NetChui choose a server?",
  "faqA24": "By default, NetChui selects the server with the lowest latency to your location. You can manually override this in Settings if you want to test connectivity to a specific region.",
  "faqQ25": "Can I change the test unit?",
  "faqA25": "Currently, NetChui displays results in Mbps (Megabits per second) as it is the industry standard for network speed. We are looking into adding a MB/s toggle in future updates.",
  "faqQ26": "Does my device case affect Wi-Fi?",
  "faqA26": "Metal or very thick plastic cases can occasionally attenuate Wi-Fi and cellular signals. If you notice significantly lower speeds, try testing without the case.",
  "faqQ27": "Why does my speed drop at night?",
  "faqA27": "Network congestion often peaks in the evening when more people in your neighborhood are streaming video. This is common on cable and fixed-wireless networks.",
  "faqQ28": "What is a 'Handshake'?",
  "faqA28": "A network handshake is the automated process of negotiation between two devices to establish a connection. High latency can make this process feel sluggish.",
  "faqQ29": "How do I read the Signal Graph?",
  "faqA29": "The graph shows your signal strength over time. A flat line means a stable connection, while sharp 'valleys' indicate interference or physical obstructions.",
  "faqQ30": "What are 'Open Ports'?",
  "faqA30": "Ports are virtual doorways for data. While some are necessary, unnecessary open ports can be a security risk. NetChui's Port Scan helps you identify these.",
